To find $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$, we will need to use the quotient rule *and* the chain rule!

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[\dfrac{u\Bigl(v(x)\Bigr)}{w(x)}\right]
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[u\Bigl(v(x)\Bigr)\right]w(x)-u\Bigl(v(x)\Bigr)w'(x)}{[w(x)]^2}\quad\gray{\text{Quotient rule}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{u'\Bigl(v(x)\Bigr)v'(x)w(x)-u\Bigl(v(x)\Bigr)w'(x)}{[w(x)]^2}\quad\gray{\text{Chain rule}}
\end{align}$

Let's differentiate $u$, $v$, and $w$:

- $u'(x)=\dfrac{1}{2\sqrt{x}}$
- $v'(x)=4x$
- $w'(x)=-\sin(x)$

Now we can plug the equations for $u$, $v$, $w$, $u'$, $v'$, *AND* $w'$ into the expression we got:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{\blueD{u'\Bigl(v(x)\Bigr)}v'(x)w(x)-\maroonC{u\Bigl(v(x)\Bigr)}w'(x)}{[w(x)]^2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\blueD{\dfrac{1}{2\sqrt{2x^2}}}4x \cos(x)-\maroonC{\sqrt{2x^2}}(-\sin(x))}{\left[ \cos(x) \right]^2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{4x\cos(x)+4x^2\sin(x)}{2\sqrt{2x^2}\cos^2(x)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{2x\cos(x)+2x^2\sin(x)}{\sqrt{2x^2}\cos^2(x)}
\end{align}$
